### Module 7 — RAMP LA (Aggregator + Gatekeeper) — Deep Dive

#### How We Got There
- Initially attempted to crawl the link live and encountered a retrieval error.
- You provided a PDF printout of the opportunity details page to allow analysis.
- RAMP functions as:
  - An aggregator + gating layer for City of LA opportunities

**Project Link:**  
- https://www.rampla.org/s/opportunity-details?id=006Ql00000Wrn9pIAB

#### What Information Was Available
- Project title: “Mulholland Drive (13150) Debris Wall”
- Opportunity ID
- Status: “Amended”
- Stage: “Posted”
- Bid method: “Construction – Whole Item”
- Solicitation type: “Task Order Solicitation (TOS)”
- Bid due date/time
- Engineer’s estimate
- Location
- Scope narrative (detailed in prose)
- Amendments list with timestamp + description
- Contact info (name + email)

#### Unique High-Signal Element: Eligibility Restrictions
- Explicitly restricted bidding:
  - only five listed contractors allowed to bid as prime
- This introduces a critical concept:
  - `bid_eligibility = restricted`
  - `eligible_prime_contractors = [...]`
- This is the clearest example that:
  - “Out for bid” ≠ “you can bid”

#### Documents
- Document names and sizes visible
- Download gated behind login:
  - “Please login to download listed documents”

#### Meetings / Job Walk
- No explicit meeting / job walk requirement visible on the public page.
- Absence of explicit language should be treated as:
  - “not detected” rather than “does not exist”

#### Extraction Implications
- RAMP modules must extract:
  - eligibility gating
  - amendment signals
  - document manifest visibility vs access
- RAMP is likely to become a key “source layer” in LA region procurement.

#### Module 7 Bottom Line
- RAMP strongly validates the need for:
  - eligibility flags as first-class data
  - clear user-facing warnings (“restricted bidding”)
  - semantic parsing of narrative scope

